BT/F1 12 Tf176.5 729 TD 0.06 Tc 0 Tw (HANDBOOK FOR FEDERAL GRAND JURORS)TjET1 g 72 724 470 2 ref 72.5 724.5 469 1 res q 72 724 470 2 reW n 72.5 725.5 m 542.5 723.5 l S 541.5 725.5 m 307.5 724.5 l S Q BT/F2 12 Tf0 g 72.5 698 TD 0 Tc ("No Bill":)TjETBT108.5 674 TD 0.728 Tw (Also referred to as "not a true bill," the "no bill" is the decision by the grand jury not to)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.103 Tc 0 Tw (indict a person.)Tj0 -24 TD -0.091 Tc (Petit Jury:)TjETBT108.5 614 TD 0 Tc 0.566 Tw (The trial jury, composed of 12 members, that hears a case after indictment and renders a)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.278 Tw (verdict or decision after hearing the prosecution's entire case and whatever evidence the defendant)Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (chooses to offer.)Tj0 -24 TD -0.088 Tc (Probable Cause:)TjETBT108.5 542 TD 0 Tc -0.411 Tw (The finding necessary in order to return an indictment against a person accused of a federal)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.26 Tw (crime. A finding of probable cause is proper only when the evidence presented to the grand jury,)Tj0 -12 TD 0.766 Tw (without any explanation being offered by the accused, persuades 12 or more grand jurors that a)TjT* -0.123 Tc 0 Tw (federal crime has probably been committed by the person accused.)Tj0 -24 TD 0 Tc (Quorum for Grand Jury to Conduct Business:)TjETBT108.5 458 TD -0.712 Tw (Sixteen of the 23 members of a federal grand jury must at all times be present at a grand jury)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.064 Tc 0 Tw (session in order for the grand jury to be able to conduct business.)Tj0 -24 TD -0.144 Tc (United States Attorney:)TjETBT108.5 398 TD -0.148 Tc (The chief legal officer for the United States government in each federal district.)TjETBT290.5 39 TD 0 Tc (Page 9)TjETBT495.5 39 TD (HB - 101)TjET
endstreamendobj2 0 obj[/PDF/Text/ImageB]endobj6 0 obj<</BaseFont/Times-Bold/Encoding/MacRomanEncoding/Name/F1/Subtype/TrueType/Type/Font>>endobj11 0 obj<</BaseFont/Times-Roman/Encoding/MacRomanEncoding/Name/F2/Subtype/TrueType/Type/Font>>endobj40 0 obj<</Length 3143>>stream
BT/F1 12 Tf176.5 729 TD 0.06 Tc 0 Tw (HANDBOOK FOR FEDERAL GRAND JURORS)TjET1 g 72 724 470 2 ref 72.5 724.5 469 1 res q 72 724 470 2 reW n 72.5 725.5 m 542.5 723.5 l S 541.5 725.5 m 307.5 724.5 l S Q BT0 g 241.5 698 TD 0 Tc (GLOSSARY OF TERMS)Tj/F2 12 Tf-169 -24 TD -0.206 Tc (Accused:)TjETBT108.5 650 TD 0 Tc (The person accused of the commission of a federal crime. Use of this term does not imply)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.105 Tw (the person under investigation is guilty of any crime. After a person is indicted by the grand jury,)Tj0 -12 TD -0.088 Tc 0 Tw (that person is referred to as the "defendant.")Tj0 -24 TD -0.065 Tc (Charge to the Grand Jury:)TjETBT108.5 578 TD 0 Tc 1.271 Tw (Given by the judge presiding over the selection and organization of the grand jury, the)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.391 Tw (charge is the court's instructions to the grand jury as to its duties, functions, and obligations, and)Tj0 -12 TD -0.119 Tc 0 Tw (how to best perform them.)Tj0 -24 TD -0.141 Tc (Deliberations:)TjETBT108.5 506 TD 0 Tc -0.366 Tw (The discussion by the grand jury members as to whether or not to return an indictment on a)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.107 Tw (given charge against an accused. During deliberations no one except the grand jury members may)Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (be present.)Tj0 -24 TD -0.148 Tc (District:)TjETBT108.5 434 TD 0 Tc -0.588 Tw (The geographical area over which the federal district court where the grand jury sits and the)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.125 Tw (grand jury itself have jurisdiction. The territorial limitations of the district will be explained to the)Tj0 -12 TD -0.1 Tc 0 Tw (grand jury by the district judge.)Tj0 -24 TD -0.331 Tc (Evidence:)TjETBT108.5 362 TD 0 Tc 1.724 Tw (Testimony of witnesses, documents, and exhibits as presented to the grand jury by an)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.228 Tw (attorney for the government or otherwise properly brought before it. In some instances, the person)Tj0 -12 TD -0.143 Tc 0 Tw (under investigation may also testify.)Tj0 -24 TD -0.165 Tc (Federal:)TjETBT108.5 290 TD -0.141 Tc (The national government as distinguished from the state governments.)Tj-36 -24 TD -0.05 Tc (Grand Jurors' Immunity:)TjETBT108.5 242 TD 0 Tc -0.946 Tw (Immunity is granted to all grand jurors for their authorized actions while serving on a federal)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.548 Tw (grand jury and means that no grand juror may be penalized for actions taken within the scope of his)Tj0 -12 TD -0.102 Tc 0 Tw (or her service as a grand juror.)Tj0 -24 TD -0.211 Tc (Indictment:)TjETBT108.5 170 TD 0 Tc 0.531 Tw (The written formal charge of a crime by the grand jury, returned when 12 or more grand)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.147 Tc 0 Tw (jurors vote in favor of it.)Tj0 -24 TD -0.138 Tc (Information:)TjETBT108.5 110 TD 0 Tc -0.203 Tw (The written formal charge of crime by the United States Attorney, filed against an accused)Tj-36 -12 TD -1.434 Tw (who, if charged with a serious crime, must have knowingly waived the requirements that the evidence)Tj0 -12 TD -0.084 Tc 0 Tw (first be presented to a grand jury.)TjETBT290.5 39 TD 0 Tc (Page 8)TjETBT495.5 39 TD (HB - 101)TjET
endstreamendobj37 0 obj<</Length 2845>>stream
BT/F1 12 Tf176.5 729 TD 0.06 Tc 0 Tw (HANDBOOK FOR FEDERAL GRAND JURORS)TjET1 g 72 724 470 2 ref 72.5 724.5 469 1 res q 72 724 470 2 reW n 72.5 725.5 m 542.5 723.5 l S 541.5 725.5 m 307.5 724.5 l S Q BT/F2 12 Tf0 g 108.5 698 TD 0 Tc -0.517 Tw (Witnesses should be treated courteously when they appear before the grand jury. Questions)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.387 Tw (should be put to them in an orderly fashion. The United States Attorney should complete his or her)Tj0 -12 TD -0.145 Tw (questioning of each witness before the foreperson asks questions. The remaining grand jurors will)TjT* -0.141 Tc 0 Tw (then have a chance to ask relevant and proper questions.)TjETBT108.5 638 TD 0 Tc -0.28 Tw (Each grand juror has an equal voice in determining whether or not an indictment should be)Tj-36 -12 TD -1.074 Tw (returned. Therefore, it is important that all grand jurors pay close attention to the testimony and other)Tj0 -12 TD -0.19 Tc 0 Tw (evidence presented.)TjETBT108.5 590 TD 0 Tc -0.159 Tw (Each grand juror must be absolutely fair in his or her judgment of the facts. Otherwise, the)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.123 Tc 0 Tw (grand juror will defeat the democratic purpose the grand jury is designed to serve.)TjETBT108.5 554 TD 0 Tc 1.61 Tw (During deliberations on a case, each grand juror should feel free to express his or her)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.134 Tc 0 Tw (opinion based upon the evidence.)TjETBT108.5 518 TD 0 Tc -0.259 Tw (Each juror has equal duties and responsibilities, and each is entitled to be satisfied with the)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.31 Tw (evidence before being called upon to vote. No juror has the right to dismiss a witness or to shut off)Tj0 -12 TD -0.077 Tc 0 Tw (proper discussion if other jurors wish to pursue the matter further.)TjETBT108.5 470 TD 0 Tc 1.731 Tw (No grand jury should undertake to investigate matters outside its proper scope merely)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.148 Tc 0 Tw (because someone suggested an investigation, or because the investigation would be interesting.)TjETBT108.5 434 TD 0 Tc 1.147 Tw (No grand juror should discuss the cases under investigation with anyone, except fellow)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.416 Tw (grand jurors and the United States .Attorney or the Assistant United States Attorney, and then only)Tj0 -12 TD -0.114 Tc 0 Tw (in the grand jury room. Of course, the grand jurors may always seek the advice of the judge.)TjETBT108.5 386 TD 0 Tc 0.32 Tw (Finally, every citizen who is selected to serve on a federal grand jury should bring to this)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.656 Tw (task the determination to participate in a responsible manner and to make every effort to ensure that)Tj0 -12 TD -0.126 Tc 0 Tw (the grand jury will be a credit not only to the community it represents but to the United States.)TjETBT290.5 39 TD 0 Tc (Page 7)TjETBT495.5 39 TD (HB - 101)TjET
endstreamendobj34 0 obj<</Length 4230>>stream
BT/F1 12 Tf176.5 729 TD 0.06 Tc 0 Tw (HANDBOOK FOR FEDERAL GRAND JURORS)TjET1 g 72 724 470 2 ref 72.5 724.5 469 1 res q 72 724 470 2 reW n 72.5 725.5 m 542.5 723.5 l S 541.5 725.5 m 307.5 724.5 l S Q BT/F2 12 Tf0 g 108.5 698 TD 0 Tc (The foreperson of the grand jury must keep a record of the number of jurors concurring in)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.1 Tw (the finding of every indictment and file the record with the Clerk of the Court. If an indictment is)Tj0 -12 TD -0.327 Tw (found, the grand jury will report it to the judge or a magistrate in open court. It will likewise report)TjT* 1.35 Tw (any "not true bills," or decisions not to indict. A decision not to indict should immediately be)TjT* -0.795 Tw (reported to the court in writing by the foreperson so that the accused may promptly be released from)TjT* -0.137 Tc 0 Tw (jail or freed from bail.)Tj/F1 12 Tf205 -24 TD 0 Tc (SECRECY)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 590 TD 0.936 Tw (The law imposes upon each grand juror a strict obligation of secrecy. This obligation is)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.124 Tc 0 Tw (emphasized in the oath each grand juror takes and in the charge given to the grand jury by the judge.)TjETBT108.5 554 TD 0 Tc -0.429 Tw (The tradition of secrecy continues as a vital part of the grand jury system for many reasons.)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.632 Tw (It protects the grand jurors from being subjected to pressure by persons who may be subjects of)Tj0 -12 TD 1.563 Tw (investigations by the grand jury or associates of such persons. It prevents the escape of those)TjT* 0.081 Tw (against whom an indictment is being considered. It encourages witnesses before the grand jury to)TjT* -0.895 Tw (give full and truthful information as to the commission of a crime. It also prevents tampering with or)TjT* 1.388 Tw (intimidation of such witnesses before they testify at trial. Finally, it prevents the disclosure of)TjT* -1.033 Tw (investigations that result in no action by the grand jury and avoids any stigma the public might attach)TjT* -0.126 Tc 0 Tw (to one who is the subject of a mere investigation by the grand jury.)TjETBT108.5 446 TD 0 Tc -0.586 Tw (Essentially, the grand jury may disclose matters occurring before it only to the attorneys for)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.321 Tw (the government for use in the performance of their duties, but even attorneys for the government)Tj0 -12 TD -0.232 Tw (may not be informed of what took place during the grand jury's deliberations and voting. The only)TjT* -0.106 Tc 0 Tw (other time matters occurring before the grand jury may be disclosed to anyone is when disclosure is)TjT* 0 Tc 0.479 Tw (ordered by the court in the interests of justice. Disclosure of such matters may never be made to)TjT* -0.075 Tc 0 Tw (grand juror's friends or family, including a grand juror's spouse.)Tj/F1 12 Tf135 -24 TD 0 Tc (PROTECTION OF GRAND JURORS)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 338 TD -0.814 Tw (The secrecy imposed upon grand jurors is a major source of protection for them. In addition,)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.589 Tw (no inquiry may be made to learn what grand jurors said or how they voted, except upon order of the)Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (court.)TjETBT108.5 290 TD -0.141 Tc (The law gives the members of a grand jury broad immunity for actions taken by them within)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.08 Tc (the scope of their authority as grand jurors.)TjETBT108.5 254 TD 0 Tc -0.256 Tw (Because of this immunity, all grand jurors must perform their duties with the highest sense)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.074 Tc 0 Tw (of responsibility.)Tj/F1 12 Tf90 -24 TD 0.06 Tc (PRACTICAL SUGGESTIONS FOR GRAND JURORS)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 194 TD 0 Tc 0.364 Tw (Each grand juror should attend the grand jury sessions regularly, in order to ensure that a)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.082 Tc 0 Tw (quorum of 16 members will be present to conduct the grand jury's business.)TjETBT108.5 158 TD -0.12 Tc (Each grand juror should be on time for each meeting so that others are not kept waiting.)TjETBT108.5 134 TD 0 Tc 0.778 Tw (The time of meetings should be scheduled so as to be convenient for the grand jury, the)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.142 Tc 0 Tw (United States Attorney, and the witnesses.)TjETBT290.5 39 TD 0 Tc (Page 6)TjETBT495.5 39 TD (HB - 101)TjET
endstreamendobj31 0 obj<</Length 4912>>stream
BT/F1 12 Tf176.5 729 TD 0.06 Tc 0 Tw (HANDBOOK FOR FEDERAL GRAND JURORS)TjET1 g 72 724 470 2 ref 72.5 724.5 469 1 res q 72 724 470 2 reW n 72.5 725.5 m 542.5 723.5 l S 541.5 725.5 m 307.5 724.5 l S Q BT/F2 12 Tf0 g 108.5 698 TD 0 Tc 2.212 Tw (Additionally, a witness who is appearing before the grand jury may invoke the Fifth)Tj-36 -12 TD -1.041 Tw (Amendment privilege against self-incrimination and refuse to answer a question. In such a situation,)Tj0 -12 TD -0.726 Tw (the grand jurors may bring the matter before the court in order to obtain a ruling as to whether or not)TjT* -0.14 Tc 0 Tw (the answer may be compelled. One manner in which an answer may be compelled is by granting the)TjT* -0.12 Tc (witness immunity from prosecution in exchange for the witness' testimony.)Tj/F1 12 Tf0 -24 TD 0.139 Tc (\(4\) Calling the Person Under Investigation as a Witness)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 602 TD 0 Tc 0.243 Tw (Normally, neither the person under investigation \(sometimes referred to as the "accused,")Tj-36 -12 TD 0.922 Tw (although this does not imply he or she is guilty of any crime\) nor any witness on the accused's)Tj0 -12 TD -0.133 Tc 0 Tw (behalf will testify before the grand jury.)TjETBT108.5 554 TD 0 Tc -0.229 Tw (Upon request, preferably in writing, an accused may be given the opportunity by the grand)Tj-36 -12 TD 0 Tw (jury to appear before it. An accused who does so appear cannot be forced to testify because of the)Tj0 -12 TD -0.604 Tw (constitutional privilege against self-incrimination. If the grand jury attempts to force the accused to)TjT* -0.148 Tc 0 Tw (testify, an indictment returned against that person may be nullified.)TjETBT108.5 494 TD 0 Tc 0.606 Tw (Because the appearance of an accused before the grand jury may raise complicated legal)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.675 Tw (problems, a grand jury that desires to request or to permit an accused to appear before it should)Tj0 -12 TD -0.14 Tc 0 Tw (consult with the United States Attorney and, if necessary, the court before proceeding.)TjETBT108.5 446 TD 0 Tc -0.543 Tw (Even if the accused is willing to testify voluntarily, it is recommended that he or she first be)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.306 Tw (warned of the right not to testify. Also, he or she may be required to sign a formal waiver of this)Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (right. The grand jury should be completely satisfied that the accused fully understands what he or)TjT* (she is doing.)Tj/F1 12 Tf0 -24 TD 0.053 Tc (\(5\) The Evidence Needed Before a "'True Bill" May Be Voted)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 362 TD 0 Tc 0.732 Tw (It is the responsibility of the grand jury to weigh the evidence presented to it in order to)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.735 Tw (determine whether this evidence, usually without any explanation being offered by the accused,)Tj0 -12 TD 0.579 Tw (persuades it that there is probable cause to believe that a crime has been committed and that the)TjT* 0.941 Tw (accused was the person who committed it. Remember that the grand jury is not responsible for)TjT* 0.795 Tw (determining whether the accused is guilty beyond a reasonable doubt, but only whether there is)TjT* 0.94 Tw (sufficient evidence of probable cause to justify bringing the accused to trial. Only the evidence)TjT* -0.913 Tw (presented to the grand jury in the grand jury room may be considered in determining whether to vote)TjT* -0.189 Tc 0 Tw (an indictment.)Tj/F1 12 Tf0 -24 TD 0.256 Tc (\(6\) Deliberations)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 230 TD 0 Tc -0.301 Tw (When the grand jury has received all the evidence on a given charge, all persons other than)Tj-36 -12 TD 2.928 Tw (the members of the grand jury must leave the room so that the grand jury may begin its)Tj0 -12 TD 1.965 Tw (deliberations. The presence of any other person in the grand jury room while the grand jury)TjT* -0.147 Tc 0 Tw (deliberates or votes may nullify an indictment returned on the accusation.)TjETBT108.5 170 TD 0 Tc -0.257 Tw (After all persons other than the grand jury members have left the room, the foreperson will)Tj-36 -12 TD 2.296 Tw (ask the grand jury members to discuss and vote upon the question of whether the evidence)Tj0 -12 TD -0.24 Tw (persuades the grand jury that a crime has probably been committed by the person accused and that)TjT* 0 Tw (an indictment should be returned. Every grand juror has the right to express his or her view of the)TjT* -0.57 Tw (matter under consideration, and grand jurors should listen to the comments of all their fellow grand)TjT* 0 Tw (jurors before making up their mind. Only after each grand juror has been given the opportunity to)TjT* -0.378 Tw (be heard will the vote be taken. It should be remembered that at least 16 jurors must be present and)TjT* -0.147 Tc 0 Tw (12 members must vote in favor of the indictment before it may be returned.)TjETBT290.5 39 TD 0 Tc (Page 5)TjETBT495.5 39 TD (HB - 101)TjET
endstreamendobj4 0 obj<</Contents[9 0 R 12 0 R]/Parent 5 0 R/Resources<</Font<</F1 6 0 R/F2 11 0 R>>/ProcSet 2 0 R/XObject<</I1 7 0 R>>>>/Type/Page>>endobj14 0 obj<</Contents 15 0 R/Parent 5 0 R/Resources<</Font<</F1 6 0 R/F2 11 0 R>>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Type/Page>>endobj17 0 obj<</Contents 18 0 R/Parent 5 0 R/Resources<</Font<</F1 6 0 R/F2 11 0 R>>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Type/Page>>endobj20 0 obj<</Contents 21 0 R/Parent 5 0 R/Resources<</Font<</F1 6 0 R/F2 11 0 R>>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Type/Page>>endobj23 0 obj<</Contents 24 0 R/Parent 5 0 R/Resources<</Font<</F1 6 0 R/F2 11 0 R>>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Type/Page>>endobj26 0 obj<</Contents 27 0 R/Parent 5 0 R/Resources<</Font<</F1 6 0 R/F2 11 0 R>>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Type/Page>>endobj27 0 obj<</Length 4870>>stream
BT/F1 12 Tf176.5 729 TD 0.06 Tc 0 Tw (HANDBOOK FOR FEDERAL GRAND JURORS)TjET1 g 72 724 470 2 ref 72.5 724.5 469 1 res q 72 724 470 2 reW n 72.5 725.5 m 542.5 723.5 l S 541.5 725.5 m 307.5 724.5 l S Q BT0 g 268.5 698 TD 0 Tc (PROCEDURE)Tj-196 -24 TD 0.2 Tc (\(1\) Quorum)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 650 TD 0 Tc 1.23 Tw (Sixteen of the 23 members of the grand jury constitute a quorum for the transaction of)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.322 Tw (business. If fewer than this number are present, even for a moment, the proceedings of the grand)Tj0 -12 TD 0.982 Tw (jury must stop. This shows how important it is that each grand juror conscientiously attend the)TjT* 0.458 Tw (meetings. If an emergency will prevent a grand juror's attendance at the meeting, he or she must)TjT* 0.177 Tw (promptly advise the grand jury foreperson. If the juror's absence will prevent the grand jury from)TjT* -0.163 Tc 0 Tw (acting, the grand juror should, if at all possible, attend the meeting.)Tj/F1 12 Tf0 -24 TD 0.04 Tc (\(2\) Evidence Before the Grand Jury)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 542 TD 0 Tc 1.762 Tw (Much of the grand jury's time is spent hearing testimony by witnesses and examining)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.162 Tc 0 Tw (documentary or other evidence in order to determine whether such evidence justifies an indictment.)TjETBT108.5 506 TD 0 Tc 0.786 Tw (Each federal court district has a United States Attorney whose duty it is to represent the)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.611 Tw (United States in federal matters within the district and to prosecute those accused of federal crimes.)Tj0 -12 TD 0.276 Tw (In the usual case, the United States Attorney or one of the Assistant United States Attorneys will)TjT* -0.136 Tw (present the evidence of alleged violations of the law to the grand jury. These attorneys also advise)TjT* 1.485 Tw (grand jurors as to what witnesses should be called and what documentary evidence should be)TjT* 0.231 Tw (produced for examination by the grand jury. The grand jury may ask that additional witnesses be)TjT* -0.212 Tw (called if it believes this necessary. The United States Attorney will also prepare the formal written)TjT* 0.362 Tw (indictments that the grand jury wishes to present. But neither the United States Attorney nor any)TjT* -0.726 Tw (Assistant United States Attorney may remain in the room while the grand jury deliberates and votes)TjT* -0.156 Tc 0 Tw (on an indictment.)Tj/F1 12 Tf0 -24 TD 0.197 Tc (\(3\) Questioning the Witness)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 350 TD 0 Tc 0.55 Tw (Witnesses are called to testify one after another. Upon appearing to give testimony, each)Tj-36 -12 TD 1.308 Tw (witness will be sworn by the grand jury foreperson or, in the foreperson's absence, the deputy)Tj0 -12 TD 2.038 Tw (foreperson. The witness will then be questioned. Ordinarily, the attorney for the government)TjT* 1.474 Tw (questions the witness first, followed next by the foreperson of the grand jury. Then, the other)TjT* -0.104 Tc 0 Tw (members of the grand jury may question the witness.)TjETBT108.5 278 TD 0 Tc 0.23 Tw (All questions asked of each witness must be relevant and proper, relating only to the case)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.159 Tw (under investigation. If doubt should arise as to whether a question is appropriate, the advice of the)Tj0 -12 TD -0.115 Tc 0 Tw (United States Attorney may be sought. If necessary, a ruling may be obtained from the court.)TjETBT108.5 230 TD 0 Tc (Because of the need for secrecy, described in more detail in the following section, the law)Tj-36 -12 TD 1.247 Tw (forbids anyone other than authorized persons from being present in the grand jury room while)Tj0 -12 TD -0.326 Tw (evidence is being presented. This means that only the grand jury, the United States Attorney or the)TjT* 2.37 Tw (Assistant United States Attorney, the witness under examination, the court reporter, and the)TjT* 0.321 Tw (interpreter \(if the foreperson determines one is required\) may be present. If an indictment should)TjT* -0.15 Tc 0 Tw (ultimately be voted, the presence of unauthorized persons in the grand jury room could invalidate it.)TjETBT108.5 146 TD 0 Tc -0.592 Tw (Occasionally, prior to answering a question, a witness may ask to leave the grand jury room)Tj-36 -12 TD 1.459 Tw (to consult with his or her attorney. The grand jury is to draw no adverse inference from such)Tj0 -12 TD 0.695 Tw (conduct, for every witness has the right to confer with counsel even though counsel may not be)TjT* 0.244 Tw (present in the grand jury room. In fact, a witness may confer with counsel after each question, as)TjT* 1.287 Tw (long as he or she does not make a mockery of the proceedings or does not, by such, make an)TjT* -0.126 Tc 0 Tw (attempt to impede the orderly progress of the grand jury investigation.)TjETBT290.5 39 TD 0 Tc (Page 4)TjETBT495.5 39 TD (HB - 101)TjET
endstreamendobj24 0 obj<</Length 4245>>stream
BT/F1 12 Tf176.5 729 TD 0.06 Tc 0 Tw (HANDBOOK FOR FEDERAL GRAND JURORS)TjET1 g 72 724 470 2 ref 72.5 724.5 469 1 res q 72 724 470 2 reW n 72.5 725.5 m 542.5 723.5 l S 541.5 725.5 m 307.5 724.5 l S Q BT/F2 12 Tf0 g 108.5 698 TD -0.117 Tc (It should be borne in mind that a federal grand jury can take action only upon federal crimes)Tj-36 -12 TD 0 Tc -0.59 Tw (that have been committed within the district in which it has been impaneled. Furthermore, a federal)Tj0 -12 TD -0.081 Tc 0 Tw (grand jury \(except a special grand jury impaneled under 18 U.S.C. \244\244 3331-3334\) is not authorized)TjT* 0 Tc -1.438 Tw (to investigate situations involving the conduct of individuals, public officials, agencies or institutions)TjT* 0.26 Tw (that the grand jury believes is subject to mere criticism rather than a violation of federal criminal)TjT* -1.189 Tw (statutes. Its concern must be devoted solely to ascertaining whether there is probable cause to believe)TjT* -0.146 Tc 0 Tw (that a federal crime has been committed and to report accordingly to the court.)Tj/F1 12 Tf140 -24 TD 0.052 Tc (SELECTION OF GRAND JURORS)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 578 TD 0 Tc -0.284 Tw (Federal law requires that a grand jury be selected at random from a fair cross section of the)Tj-36 -12 TD -1.242 Tw (community in the district or division in which the federal grand jury convenes. Thus, all citizens have)Tj0 -12 TD -0.132 Tc 0 Tw (an equal opportunity and obligation to serve.)TjETBT108.5 530 TD 0 Tc 0.452 Tw (Pursuant to law, the names of prospective grand jurors are drawn at random from lists of)Tj-36 -12 TD 1.986 Tw (registered voters or lists of actual voters, or other sources when necessary, under procedures)Tj0 -12 TD -0.463 Tw (designed to ensure that all groups in the community will have a fair chance to serve. Those persons)TjT* -0.156 Tw (whose names have been drawn and who are not exempt or excused from service are summoned to)TjT* 0.433 Tw (appear for duty as grand jurors. When these persons appear before the court, the presiding judge)TjT* 0.988 Tw (may consider any further requests to be excused. The judge will then direct the selection of 23)TjT* -0.102 Tc 0 Tw (qualified persons to become the members of the grand jury.)Tj/F1 12 Tf108 -24 TD -0.039 Tc (ORGANIZATION, OATH, AND OFFICERS OF)Tj42 -12 TD 0.06 Tc (THE FEDERAL GRAND JURY)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 398 TD 0 Tc 1.51 Tw (After the proper number of persons have been qualified as grand jurors, the court will)Tj-36 -12 TD 2.362 Tw (appoint one of them to be the foreperson, or presiding officer, of the grand jury. A deputy)Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (foreperson will also be appointed, so that he or she can act as presiding officer in the foreperson's)TjT* (absence.)TjETBT108.5 338 TD 0.602 Tw (The foreperson, the deputy foreperson, and the remaining members of the grand jury are)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.088 Tc 0 Tw (sworn in by the Clerk of the Court. Those persons who do not wish to swear may affirm.)TjETBT108.5 302 TD 0 Tc (The oath taken by the grand jurors binds them to inquire diligently and objectively into all)Tj-36 -12 TD 1.01 Tw (federal crimes committed within the district of which they have or may obtain evidence and to)Tj0 -12 TD -0.175 Tc 0 Tw (conduct such inquiry without malice, fear, hatred, or other emotion.)TjETBT108.5 254 TD 0 Tc 0.674 Tw (After the grand jurors have been sworn, the presiding judge advises the grand jury of its)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.065 Tw (obligations and how best to perform its duties. This is called the charge to the grand jury. Careful)Tj0 -12 TD -0.271 Tw (attention must be paid to the charge, for it and any additional instructions that may be given by the)TjT* -0.121 Tc 0 Tw (court contain the rules and directions the grand jury must follow during its term of service.)TjETBT108.5 194 TD 0 Tc -0.177 Tw (After the grand jury has been charged, it is taken to the grand jury room, where it will hear)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.485 Tw (testimony and consider documentary evidence in the cases brought to its attention by the United)Tj0 -12 TD -0.12 Tc 0 Tw (States Attorney or an Assistant United States Attorney.)TjETBT290.5 39 TD 0 Tc (Page 3)TjETBT495.5 39 TD (HB - 101)TjET
endstreamendobj21 0 obj<</Length 5023>>stream
BT/F1 12 Tf176.5 729 TD 0.06 Tc 0 Tw (HANDBOOK FOR FEDERAL GRAND JURORS)TjET1 g 72 724 470 2 ref 72.5 724.5 469 1 res q 72 724 470 2 reW n 72.5 725.5 m 542.5 723.5 l S 541.5 725.5 m 307.5 724.5 l S Q BT0 g 215.5 698 TD 0.055 Tc (NATURE OF THE GRAND JURY)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 674 TD -0.114 Tc (The powers and functions of the federal grand jury differ from those of the federal trial jury,)Tj-36 -12 TD 0 Tc -0.899 Tw (which is called the petit jury. The petit jury listens to the evidence offered by the prosecution and the)Tj0 -12 TD -0.799 Tw (defense \(if it chooses to offer any\) during a criminal trial and returns a verdict of guilty or not guilty.)TjT* -0.306 Tw (The grand jury, on the other hand, does not determine guilt or innocence, but only whether there is)TjT* 2.211 Tw (probable cause to believe that a crime was committed and that a specific person or persons)TjT* -0.771 Tw (committed it. If the grand jury finds probable cause to exist, then it will return a written statement of)TjT* -0.178 Tc 0 Tw (the charges called an "indictment." After that, the accused will go to trial.)TjETBT108.5 578 TD 0 Tc 3.062 Tw (The grand jury normally hears only that evidence presented by an attorney for the)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.126 Tw (government which tends to show the commission of a crime. The grand jury must determine from)Tj0 -12 TD -1.123 Tw (this evidence, and usually without hearing evidence for the defense, whether a person should be tried)TjT* 0.236 Tw (for a serious federal crime, referred to in the Bill of Rights as an "infamous crime." An infamous)TjT* -0.548 Tw (crime is one which may be punished by imprisonment for more than one year. As a general rule, no)TjT* 0.814 Tw (one can be prosecuted for a serious crime unless the grand jury decides that the evidence it has)TjT* 2.955 Tw (heard so requires. In this way, the grand jury operates both as a "sword," authorizing the)TjT* 0.458 Tw (government's prosecution of suspected criminals, and also as a "shield," protecting citizens from)TjT* -0.41 Tw (unwarranted or inappropriate prosecutions. A person may, however, waive grand jury proceedings)TjT* -0.143 Tc 0 Tw (and agree to be prosecuted by a written charge of crime called an information.)TjETBT108.5 446 TD 0 Tc 0.654 Tw (The grand jury is not completely free to compel a trial of anyone it chooses. The United)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.42 Tw (States Attorney must sign the indictment before one may be prosecuted. Thus, the government and)Tj0 -12 TD 0.698 Tw (the grand jury act as checks upon each other. This assures that neither may arbitrarily wield the)TjT* -0.142 Tc 0 Tw (awesome power to indict a person of a crime.)Tj/F1 12 Tf0 -24 TD 0.114 Tc (\(1\) The Grand Jury's Tasks)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 362 TD 0 Tc -0.179 Tw (As stated above, the federal grand jury's function is to determine whether a person shall be)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.614 Tw (tried for a serious federal crime alleged to have been committed within the district where it sits.)Tj0 -12 TD 1.241 Tw (Matters may be brought to its attention in three ways: \(1\) by the United States Attorney or an)TjT* 1.229 Tw (Assistant United States Attorney; \(2\) by the court that impaneled it; and \(3\) from the personal)TjT* -0.418 Tw (knowledge of a member of the grand jury or from matters properly brought to a member's personal)TjT* -0.149 Tc 0 Tw (attention. In all these cases, the grand jury must hear evidence before taking action.)TjETBT108.5 278 TD 0 Tc 1.083 Tw (After it has received evidence against a person, the grand jury must decide whether the)Tj-36 -12 TD -1.402 Tw (evidence presented justifies an indictment, or "true bill," which is the formal criminal charge returned)Tj0 -12 TD -0.169 Tw (by the grand jury. Upon the indictment's being filed in court, the person accused must either plead)TjT* -0.113 Tc 0 Tw (guilty or nolo contendere or stand trial.)TjETBT108.5 218 TD 0 Tc (If the evidence does not persuade the grand jury that there is probable cause to believe the)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.692 Tw (person committed a crime, the grand jury will vote a "no bill," or "not a true bill." When this occurs,)Tj0 -12 TD -0.127 Tc 0 Tw (the person is not required to plead to a criminal charge, and no trial is required.)Tj/F1 12 Tf0 -24 TD 0.196 Tc (\(2\) Investigation)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 146 TD 0 Tc 1.901 Tw (The major portion of the grand jury's work is concerned with evidence brought to its)Tj-36 -12 TD 1.959 Tw (attention by an attorney for the government. The grand jury may consider additional matters)Tj0 -12 TD 0.138 Tw (otherwise brought to its attention, but should consult with the United States Attorney or the court)TjT* -0.489 Tw (before undertaking a formal investigation of such matters. This is necessary because the grand jury)TjT* -0.177 Tc 0 Tw (has no investigative staff, and legal assistance will be necessary in the event an indictment is voted.)TjETBT290.5 39 TD 0 Tc (Page 2)TjETBT495.5 39 TD (HB - 101)TjET
endstreamendobj18 0 obj<</Length 5261>>stream
BT/F1 12 Tf176.5 729 TD 0.06 Tc 0 Tw (HANDBOOK FOR FEDERAL GRAND JURORS)TjET1 g 72 724 470 2 ref 72.5 724.5 469 1 res q 72 724 470 2 reW n 72.5 725.5 m 542.5 723.5 l S 541.5 725.5 m 307.5 724.5 l S Q BT0 g 216.5 698 TD -0.07 Tc (PURPOSE OF THIS HANDBOOK)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 674 TD 0 Tc 0.391 Tw (This Handbook will acquaint persons who have been selected to serve on a federal grand)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.519 Tw (jury with the general nature and importance of their role as grand jurors. It explains some of the)Tj0 -12 TD 0.199 Tw (terms that grand jurors will encounter during their service and offers some suggestions helpful to)TjT* -0.723 Tw (them in performing this important public service. It is intended that this Handbook will, to a degree,)TjT* 1.234 Tw (repeat and provide a permanent record of much of the information presented in the grand jury)TjT* 1.009 Tw (orientation film, )Tj/F2 12 Tf83 0 TD 2.096 Tw (The People's Panel)TjET0.5 w 155.5 612.2 m 250.5 612.2 l S 1 w BT/F2 12 Tf250.5 614 TD 2.427 Tw (, which in most districts is shown to grand jurors at the)Tj-178 -12 TD -0.755 Tw (commencement of their service. Grand jurors are encouraged to refer to this Handbook periodically)Tj0 -12 TD -0.149 Tc 0 Tw (throughout their service to reacquaint themselves with their duties and responsibilities.)TjETBT108.5 566 TD 0 Tc 0.895 Tw (This Handbook is designed as an aid only to persons serving on a federal - not a state -)Tj-36 -12 TD -1.011 Tw (grand jury. The federal grand jury is concerned only with federal crimes; it derives its authority from)Tj0 -12 TD (the Constitution of the United States, national laws, and the rules of the federal courts. There are also)TjT* 0.23 Tw (grand juries impaneled in many of the states, but those grand juries investigate only state crimes;)TjT* -1.031 Tw (they derive their authority from the constitutions, laws, and rules of court of the states where they are)TjT* -0.198 Tc 0 Tw (impaneled.)Tj/F1 12 Tf104 -24 TD -0.047 Tc (ORIGIN AND HISTORY OF THE GRAND JURY)TjETBT/F2 12 Tf108.5 458 TD 0 Tc -0.783 Tw (The grand jury has a long and honorable tradition. It was recognized in the Magna Carta, the)Tj-36 -12 TD 1.695 Tw (first English constitutional document, which King John granted in 1215 at the demand of his)Tj0 -12 TD 0.407 Tw (subjects. The first English grand jury consisted of twelve men selected from the knights or other)TjT* -0.181 Tc 0 Tw (freemen, who were summoned to inquire into crimes alleged to have been committed in their local)TjT* -0.105 Tc (community. Thus, grand jurors originally functioned as accusers or witnesses, rather than as judges.)TjETBT108.5 386 TD 0 Tc -0.729 Tw (Over the years, the hallmarks of our modern grand jury developed in England. For example,)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.144 Tw (grand jury proceedings became secret, and the grand jury became independent of the Crown. As a)Tj0 -12 TD -0.877 Tw (result, a grand jury is able to vote an indictment or refuse to do so, as it deems proper, without regard)TjT* -0.538 Tw (to the recommendations of judge, prosecutor, or any other person. This independence from the will)TjT* 1.616 Tw (of the government was achieved only after a long hard fight. It can best be illustrated by the)TjT* -0.525 Tw (celebrated English case involving the Earl of Shaftasbury, who, in 1681, fell under the suspicion of)TjT* 2.517 Tw (the Crown. Displeased with him, the Crown presented to the grand jury a proposed bill of)TjT* 1.464 Tw (indictment for high treason and recommended that it be voted and returned. After hearing the)TjT* -0.15 Tc 0 Tw (witnesses, the grand jury voted against the bill of indictment and returned it to the King, holding that)TjT* -0.145 Tc (it was not true.)TjETBT108.5 254 TD 0 Tc 2.828 Tw (When the English colonists came to America, they brought with them many of the)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.307 Tw (institutions of the English legal system, including the grand jury. Thus, the English tradition of the)Tj0 -12 TD 0.626 Tw (grand jury was well established in the American colonies long before the American Revolution.)TjT* 1.964 Tw (Indeed, the colonists used it as a platform from which to assert their independence from the)TjT* 2.368 Tw (pressures of colonial governors. In 1735, for example, the Colonial Governor of New York)TjT* -0.357 Tw (demanded that a grand jury indict for libel John Zenger, editor of a newspaper called "The Weekly)TjT* -0.298 Tw (Journal," because he had held up to scorn certain acts of the Royal Governor. The grand jury flatly)TjT* 0 Tw (refused.)TjETBT108.5 146 TD (The grand jury as an institution was so firmly established in the traditions of our forebears)Tj-36 -12 TD 0.121 Tw (that they included it in the Bill of Rights. The Fifth Amendment to the Constitution of the United)Tj0 -12 TD -0.555 Tw (States provides in part that "\(n\)o person shall be held to answer for a capital, or otherwise infamous)TjT* 1.216 Tw (crime, unless on a presentment or indictment of a Grand Jury . . . ." Moreover, the grand jury)TjT* -0.089 Tc 0 Tw (system is also recognized in the constitutions of many of the states of the Union.)TjETBT290.5 39 TD 0 Tc (Page 1)TjETBT495.5 39 TD (HB - 101)TjET
